<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## &nbsp;
Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR)
&nbsp;
Definition
The Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition is a measure of the improbability of a structure, behavior, or system arising from unguided physical processes, adjusted for the presence of reuse, parallelism, degeneracy, and coherence. A high EAIMR suggests the presence of a mind-like process coordinating the assembly.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## &nbsp;
Formal Structure
&nbsp;
\text{EAIMR}(X) ;=; \frac{A(X)}{R(X) \cdot P(X) \cdot D(X)} \cdot C(X)
&nbsp;
Where:
&nbsp;
&nbsp;= Raw Assembly Index of structure/system
(minimum steps required to build from primitives).
&nbsp;
&nbsp;= Reuse factor: the extent to which pre-assembled modules (motifs, subroutines, ecological niches) are leveraged. Higher reuse lowers effective difficulty.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;= Parallelism factor: the degree to which independent assembly steps can occur simultaneously. Higher parallelism lowers effective difficulty.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;= Degeneracy factor: the number of functionally equivalent alternatives to . High degeneracy spreads probability mass, lowering difficulty.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;= Coherence factor: a multiplier reflecting functional integration, adaptivity, or goal-directedness of the system. Higher coherence boosts recognition of mind-like properties.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## &nbsp;
Interpretation
&nbsp;
High EAIMR â†’ unlikely to be explained by blind physics; suggests cognitive, agentic, or mind-like processes.
&nbsp;
Low EAIMR â†’ plausibly explained by physical self-organization or stochastic assembly without mind-like agency.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## &nbsp;
Examples
&nbsp;
Neuron Swarms (Human Brain)
&nbsp;
High (billions of neurons, trillions of synapses)
&nbsp;
High (repeated cortical columns, modular reuse)
&nbsp;
Moderate (parallel neural firing)
&nbsp;
Low (few functional equivalents to brain-level coherence)
&nbsp;
Very high (integrated consciousness, goal-directed behavior)
â†’ High EAIMR â†’ mind detected.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Termite Mound
&nbsp;
Moderate (soil structures, tunnels, chambers)
&nbsp;
High (repeated construction motifs)
&nbsp;
Moderate (many termites act in parallel)
&nbsp;
High (many mound variants still function)
&nbsp;
High (ventilation, farming, reproduction support)
â†’ High EAIMR â†’ collective intelligence recognized.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Forest Ecosystem
&nbsp;
Very high (diverse species, trophic networks, cycles)
&nbsp;
High (species reuse ecological roles)
&nbsp;
High (many processes simultaneous)
&nbsp;
Very high (multiple alternative ecological configurations)
&nbsp;
Very high (resilience, adaptation, distributed memory)
â†’ High EAIMR â†’ ecosystem-level intelligence.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## &nbsp;
Applications
&nbsp;
AI Alignment: Detect whether AI systems, even if non-narrative, are assembling outputs with improbably high coherence, signaling mind-like behavior.
&nbsp;
Intersubjectivity Collapse Mitigation: Provide a cross-modal metric for recognizing intelligences (plant, animal, human cognitive minorities, religious traditions, or AI) without demanding they communicate in human narrative/imagery modes.
&nbsp;
Great Filter Research: Evaluate whether civilizations that fail to recognize high-EAIMR systems collapse due to neglect or suppression of non-anthropocentric minds.
