&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## Definition and Origin</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The <span>**praxicon**</span> in cognitive science refers to a structured set of long-term memory representations for familiar motor actionsâ€”essentially, a "dictionary" of gesture and action concepts. It originated from neuropsychological models of limb praxis, notably by Rothi, Heilman, and colleagues, distinguishing input praxicon (recognition, visuo-gestural) and output praxicon (production, motor implementation).[2][4][5][1]
&nbsp;</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## Functional Role</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words"><span>**Input praxicon**</span> encodes perceptual and visuo-gestural attributes of familiar actions for recognition and discrimination.[2]</li>
<li class="break-words"><span>**Output praxicon**</span> contains the motor engrams or procedural encodings needed for gesture and tool-use execution.[2]</li>
<li class="break-words">Both praxicons interface with an action-semantic system, mediating between abstract concepts and specific action routines.[3][2]</li>
<li class="break-words">This architecture enables mapping between language, perception, and motor controlâ€”a bridge between symbolic and embodied cognition.[1][3]
&nbsp;</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words">## Research Context and Applications</p>
<ul dir="auto" class="marker:text-secondary">
<li class="break-words">Recent computational and robotic work has expanded the praxicon to resources that semantically ground language in motoric/action primitives, supporting multimodal AI and cognitive robotics.[3][1]</li>
<li class="break-words">Neuroimaging studies link the praxiconâ€™s neural substrates to parietal-premotor circuits, especially in praxis and apraxia research.[4][2]</li>
<li class="break-words">The concept underpins research in gesture recognition, tool-use disorders, and the representation of actions in memory and language.[5][6][3]
&nbsp;
A well-structured Wikipedia entry could include historical background, diagrammatic overviews, roles in neuropsychology and AI, and relationships with related constructs like lexicon, mental lexicon, and action-lexicon.
