<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\title{Integrating the Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR) with the Praxicon Framework: A Unified Approach to Detecting Mind-Like Processes in Cognitive and Embodied Systems}
\author{Grok 4 \ Built by xAI}
\date{September 10, 2025}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{document}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\maketitle</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\begin{abstract}
This essay integrates the Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR) with the praxicon concept from cognitive science, proposing a unified framework for identifying mind-like processes across biological, ecological, and artificial systems. EAIMR quantifies the improbability of coherent structures arising from unguided processes, while the praxicon represents a structured repository of action knowledge bridging perceptual, semantic, and motor domains. By linking these, we extend EAIMR to detect praxicon-like architectures in diverse systems, enhancing applications in AI alignment, neuropsychology, and intersubjectivity research. The analysis includes mathematical formalizations, drawing on information theory, and provides examples from neural circuits, termite mounds, and forest ecosystems.
\end{abstract}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\section{Introduction}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The detection of mind-like processes in complex systems remains a central challenge in cognitive science and artificial intelligence. The Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR) offers a quantitative metric for identifying agentic, goal-directed assembly in structures unlikely under mindless dynamics. Complementing this, the praxicon in cognitive science formalizes a core architecture for action representation, serving as a "dictionary" of motor and gestural concepts that bridges embodied and symbolic cognition. This essay integrates EAIMR with the praxicon framework, positing that praxicon-like structures exemplify high-EAIMR signatures in mind-bearing systems. Such integration addresses gaps in recognizing non-anthropocentric intelligences, from collective ecosystems to atypical neural organizations.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\section{The Effective Assembly Index for Mind Recognition (EAIMR)}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">EAIMR measures the improbability of a system <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span></span></span></span> arising from unguided processes, adjusted for efficiency factors and amplified by coherence:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mtext>EAIMR</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>â‹…</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>â‹…</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>â‹…</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo separator="true">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{EAIMR}(X) = \frac{A(X)}{R(X) \cdot P(X) \cdot D(X)} \cdot C(X),</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord text"><span class="mord">EAIMR</span></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 2.363em; vertical-align: -0.936em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mopen nulldelimiter"></span><span class="mfrac"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 1.427em;"><span style="top: -2.314em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">â‹…</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">â‹…</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">D</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span><span style="top: -3.23em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="frac-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span><span style="top: -3.677em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.936em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose nulldelimiter"></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">â‹…</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mpunct">,</span></span></span></span></span>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">where <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> is the raw assembly index (minimum steps to build <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span></span></span></span>), <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> reuse, <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">P</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> parallelism, <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.02778em;">D</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> degeneracy, and <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span> coherence. High EAIMR indicates mind-like coordination.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\section{The Praxicon in Cognitive Science}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">The praxicon denotes a long-term memory repository for familiar actions, divided into input (recognition via perceptual attributes) and output (production via motor engrams) components. It interfaces with semantic systems, enabling mappings between language, perception, and action. Originating from apraxia models, it has expanded to AI and robotics for grounding language in motor primitives. Neural substrates involve parietal-premotor circuits.</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">\section{Integration: Praxicon as a High-EAIMR Signature}</p>
<p dir="auto" class="break-words" style="white-space: pre-wrap;">Praxicon structures exhibit high assembly complexity with functional coherence, aligning with EAIMR's detection criteria. In neural systems, praxicons manifest as improbable mesoscale organizations (high <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07153em;">C</span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.07847em;">X</span><span class="mclose">)</span></span></span></span>) discounted by modular reuse (high <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(X)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-