But there may indeed be loopholes in how itâ€™s applied:
&nbsp;
Reuse of substructures: If a system has access to pre-assembled modules (e.g. repeating motifs in polymers, or standardized software libraries), the effective assembly index collapses. Complexity can be bootstrapped without â€œpaying the full cost.â€
&nbsp;
Parallelism: The index assumes a linear build sequence, but many natural processes are massively parallel. Parallel pathways can drastically shorten the real â€œtime to assembly.â€
&nbsp;
Context dependence: Some structures look astronomically unlikely in isolation, but in a rich environment (e.g. Earthâ€™s oceans, or the digital commons) precursors are already abundant. This reduces effective assembly difficulty.
&nbsp;
Degeneracy: Many distinct structures can fulfill the same function. The assembly index of one particular molecule might be huge, but life doesnâ€™t need that exact one â€” just any of a vast family. So probability mass spreads out.
&nbsp;
Information leakage: If external agents (humans, AI, evolutionary processes) leave behind â€œscaffolds,â€ then future builders inherit shortcuts. The index doesnâ€™t always account for cumulative culture.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
This is where it connects back to your concerns: intersubjectivity collapse, attention economies, and AGI arms races all exploit loopholes in assembly. Instead of painstakingly building mutual understanding, platforms shortcut through algorithmic nudges. Instead of diverse emergent intelligences, AI companies reuse training scaffolds and modules. Complexity that looks â€œhardâ€ in principle becomes trivial once scaffolding and reuse are allowed.
